    "body": "Fixes #8156\n\n`with cython.critical_section(...)` and `with lock:` disposed of their arguments after their try/finally:\n\n- `break` and `continue` leave the block through the finally clause, which jumps straight to the loop's label and skips that code, so they leaked the references held for the arguments.\n- `return` released them too early: the return statement releases all temps holding references before it jumps to the finally clause, so an object that only the block kept alive was deallocated while its critical section or its lock was still held, and the unlock then touched freed memory (see the valgrind trace in the issue). Reassigning the variable that refers to the object that contains a lock, as in `with a.lock: a = None`, had the same effect.\n\nBoth statements now keep these objects in temps allocated with `manage_ref=False`, as `WithStatNode` does for `exit_var`, so that `return` leaves them alone, and their finally clause releases them after ending the critical section or releasing the lock:\n\n- For a critical section on objects, these are the arguments: their references move from the temps that they are coerced to. An argument that refers to the temp of an enclosing `LetNode` (as in the bytearray loops that `Optimize` generates) is left as it is.\n- For a pymutex or a lock, it is the Python object that the lock lives in, found by following attributes and indexing (`obj.lock`, `obj.struct.lock`, `obj.locks[i]`). Locks reached in other ways (through a function call or `&`), and locks in `nogil` code, behave as before.\n\n`CriticalSectionExitNode` gets the up-tree reference to its critical section that its `uptree_ref_attrs` already declared.\n\nThe new tests in `tests/run/critical_sections.pyx` and `tests/run/pymutex.pyx` check, for `break`, `continue`, `return` and an exception, with one and two objects, with a pymutex critical section and with `with lock:`, when the block reassigns the variable that refers to the lock's object (also for a lock reached through a struct member and indexing), and when a generator is closed inside `with lock:`, that the objects are released, and only after the critical section or the lock: `__dealloc__` logs whether the object's pymutex, or on free-threaded builds the critical section on the object (a small C helper reads `ob_mutex`), is still held. A decorator skips them on PyPy and GraalPy, as they rely on reference counting. Without the fix, 7 of the 12 fail on 3.14 and 10 on 3.14t. (The struct in that test holds a pointer to the object's lock rather than a pymutex: pymutexes inside structs aren't constructed, which is fine for `PyMutex` but leaves them NULL where `cython.pymutex` falls back to `PyThread_type_lock`, i.e. before 3.13 and with the Limited API.)\n\nGenerated C: of the 1012 C files that `runtests.py --cython-only --backends=c` writes for the test suite, 308 change: the same files as with the previous version of this PR, plus `pymutex`, whose `with obj.lock:` statements now hold `obj`. Apart from 4 profiling and monitoring tests whose module init also differs between two runs of master (indices in trace calls), every changed function contains a critical section or a lock statement (mostly the critical section in auto-pickling's `__reduce_cython__` and in generated property accessors), or is the `tp_traverse`/`tp_dealloc` of a generator closure that now keeps the object that contains a lock across a `yield`.\n\n`with obj.lock:` and `critical_section(obj.lock)` now take and release a reference to `obj`, as `critical_section(obj)` already did: with cachegrind, about 17 more instructions per acquisition on 3.14t and 11 to 14 on 3.14, where an uncontended `with self.lock:` loop iteration took about 10 before. Critical sections on objects, `@cython.critical_section`, the generated property accessors and bytearray iteration cost the same as before.\n\nTested on Linux with GitHub's CPython builds and the CFLAGS of `Tools/ci-run.sh`, C and C++ backends:\n- `critical_sections` and `pymutex` on 3.12, 3.13t, 3.14, 3.14t, 3.15 and 3.15t, with `--limited-api` on 3.14, and on PyPy 3.11\n- the 308 tests whose generated C changes, on 3.14 (compiled Cython) and 3.14t (uncompiled, as on CI), with refnanny active\n- Cython's unit tests on 3.14 and 3.14t\n- valgrind (`PYTHONMALLOC=malloc`) on the new tests, the issue's reproducer and on reassigning the object that contains a lock\n- `runcodestyle.py` and codespell\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01GwUvhmzgfkiZ6vo4uGyesN\n",
