            "commit": {
                "author": {
                    "date": "2026-11-24T20:58:55Z",
                    "email": "pylaterreur@gmail.com",
                    "name": "Pierre-Yves Le Borgne"
                },
                "comment_count": 0,
                "committer": {
                    "date": "2026-11-24T21:37:57Z",
                    "email": "pylaterreur@gmail.com",
                    "name": "Pierre-Yves Le Borgne"
                },
                "message": "Release critical section and lock arguments on every exit, after unlocking\n\n\"with cython.critical_section(...)\" and \"with lock:\" disposed of their\narguments after their try/finally, which \"break\" and \"continue\" skip,\nso those leaked the references held for the arguments.  \"return\"\nreleased them before the finally clause, together with all other temps,\nso an object that only the block kept alive was deallocated while its\ncritical section or lock was still held, and the unlock then touched\nfreed memory.  Reassigning the variable that refers to the object that\ncontains a lock (as in \"with a.lock: a = None\") had the same effect.\n\nKeep these objects in temps allocated with manage_ref=False, as\nWithStatNode does for its \"exit_var\", and release them in the finally\nclause, after ending the critical section or releasing the lock.  For a\ncritical section on objects, these are the arguments themselves.  For a\npymutex or a lock, it is the Python object that the lock lives in,\nfound by following attributes and indexing (as in \"obj.lock\",\n\"obj.struct.lock\" or \"obj.locks[i]\").\n\nFixes #8156\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01GwUvhmzgfkiZ6vo4uGyesN",
